    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's solve exponent equations where the terms are added or subtracted.\n\nA key warning first:\n\n2^a + 2^b is NOT 2^(a+b).\n\nExponent addition works for multiplication, not ordinary addition. For a sum, the useful move is usually to factor out a common power.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "concept_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "concept_1": {
      "id": "concept_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Take 2^(x-1) + 2^(x+1).\n\nThe smaller common power is 2^(x-1).\n\n2^(x+1) = 2^(x-1) × 2^2.\n\nSo the sum is:\n2^(x-1)(1+4) = 5×2^(x-1).",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
